      "problem": "Solve the equation ${4}^{x}-{4}^{5}=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding ${4}^{5}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x}-{4}^{5} & =0 \\\\ {4}^{x}-\\cancel{{4}^{5}}+\\cancel{{4}^{5}} & =0+{4}^{5} \\\\ {4}^{x} & ={4}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(4)$, we can equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x} & ={4}^{5} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5.$",
